[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Constructors, Destructors, and Object Lifecycle in OOP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the lifecycle of an object in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is crucial for resource management, memory safety, and writing efficient applications. Every object goes through three main phases: creation, utilization, and destruction."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Object Creation and Constructors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A constructor is a special method executed automatically when an object of a class is instantiated. Its primary responsibility is to initialize the object's attributes and allocate necessary resources."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class DatabaseConnection {
public:
    DatabaseConnection() {
        // Constructor: Allocates connection
        std::cout << "Database connected." << std::endl;
    }
};"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Constructors"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default Constructor: Takes no arguments and initializes members with default values."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parameterized Constructor: Accepts arguments to initialize an object with specific states."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Copy Constructor: Creates a new object as a copy of an existing object."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Object Utilization"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"During this phase, the object resides in memory (stack or heap), and methods are invoked to perform business logic. The object remains active until it goes out of scope or is explicitly deleted."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Object Destruction and Destructors"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A destructor is automatically called when an object is destroyed or goes out of scope. It is used to release allocated memory, close file streams, and free database connections to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class DatabaseConnection {
public:
    ~DatabaseConnection() {
        // Destructor: Cleans up resources
        std::cout << "Database disconnected." << std::endl;
    }
};"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of the Object Lifecycle"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allocation: Memory is reserved for the object."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Initialization: The constructor runs to set initial values."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Execution: Methods are called during the operational phase."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Teardown: The destructor executes to release resources before memory reclamation."}]
